 ### [Adaptive Octree Marching Tetrahedra](3897-adaptive-octree-marching-tetrahedra.md)
-Uses an adaptive octree to subdivide the volume, applying Marching Tetrahedrons only at leaf nodes where the isovalue is present. Reduces computation in empty regions compared to uniform grid traversal.
+An isosurface extraction method that uses an adaptive octree to subdivide a 3D volume only where the scalar field contains the target isovalue, applying the Marching Tetrahedra algorithm at leaf nodes to generate the mesh. It differs from its parent [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md) by introducing spatial adaptivity via octree subdivision, reducing computation in empty regions, and from [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md) by avoiding uniform grid traversal. Choose it when memory efficiency in sparse volumes is critical.
 
 ### [Naive Isosurface Extraction](4328-naive-isosurface-extraction.md)
-Processes each tetrahedron independently without vertex sharing, resulting in non-manifold surfaces with cracks. Serves as a baseline for understanding the core decomposition logic.
+Naive Isosurface Extraction is a surface extraction algorithm that processes each tetrahedron in a tetrahedral mesh independently to generate a collection of individual, unconnected polygons. It solves the problem of identifying the intersection between a scalar field and a specific isovalue within a volumetric representation. Unlike optimized variants that ensure manifold connectivity through vertex reuse, this method creates new vertices for every intersection point, resulting in non-manifold surfaces with cracks at shared edges. It serves as the foundational, unoptimized baseline for understanding tetrahedral isosurface generation, building on the decomposition logic of [Marching Tetrahedrons](3720-marching-tetrahedrons.md). Choose it only for debugging or when connectivity is irrelevant.
 
