 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Doi and Koide as an alternative to Marching Cubes [S4, unofficial]. It addresses the "tetrahedron ambiguity" where a single cube can be split in two topologically distinct ways. By decomposing the cube into tetrahedra, the algorithm ensures a unique, consistent surface for each configuration [S1, unofficial]. It avoids the patent that covered Marching Cubes, which has since expired [S5]. The algorithm is significantly simpler than Marching Cubes because having only 4 points in a cell leads to merely 8 cases to consider, which can be reduced down to 3 with symmetry [S5]. It has enjoyed much research by GPGPU groups on accelerating it in hardware due to its simplicity and being essentially a triangle-driven algorithm [S5].
